									</div>
				<div class="info">
					<span class="printuser avatarhover"><a href="http://www.wikidot.com/user:info/nagiros" onclick="WIKIDOT.page.listeners.userInfo(4665703); return false;"  ><img class="small" src="http://www.wikidot.com/avatar.php?userid=4665703&amp;amp;size=small&amp;amp;timestamp=1607999106" alt="Nagiros" style="background-image:url(http://www.wikidot.com/userkarma.php?u=4665703)"/></a><a href="http://www.wikidot.com/user:info/nagiros" onclick="WIKIDOT.page.listeners.userInfo(4665703); return false;" >Nagiros</a></span> <span class="odate time_1607996042 format_%25e%20%25b%20%25Y%2C%20%25H%3A%25M%7Cagohover">15 Dec 2020 01:34</span>
																	<br/>
						in discussion <a href="/forum/c-50864/help:drafts-critique">Writing Help / Help: Drafts Critique</a> &raquo;
						<a href="/forum/t-13898044/scp-mermaids-of-the-midnight-zone">SCP Mermaids of the Midnight Zone</a>
														</div>
			</div>
			<div class="content" id="post-content-4845048">
									

<p>Was asked to take a look at this. I've got a kind of list of items I want to go over, moving from small to big, if you'll bear with me.</p>
<div class="collapsible-block">
<div class="collapsible-block-folded"><a class="collapsible-block-link" href="javascript:;">+&nbsp;show&nbsp;block</a></div>
<div class="collapsible-block-unfolded" style="display:none">
<div class="collapsible-block-unfolded-link"><a class="collapsible-block-link" href="javascript:;">–&nbsp;hide&nbsp;block</a></div>
<div class="collapsible-block-content">
<blockquote>
<p>SCP-X instances are believed to operate globally.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>This seems unlikely to me. How does this thing adapt to different sections of the oceans like that?</p>
<blockquote>
<p>In addition, all possess an exceptionally long illicium capable of extending at least 100m, and terminating into a realistic and anatomically accurate body of a human female, connected at the base of the skull.</p>
<p>These human lures act as a direct extension of SCP-X, showing them to possess corresponding intellect and the ability to speak.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>Honestly, I'd prefer if the process here were a little more imperfect, like the lure has the general shape of a woman but in daylight it's obviously some kind of growth, or their capacity for speak and intellect is rudimentary at best. It just kinda grounds the idea, I think.</p>
<blockquote>
<p>Due to SCP-X’s anomalous abilities, all tank maintenance, feedings, tests, and interviews are to be conducted solely by biologically female staff.<br />
&#8230;<br />
These effects are greatly amplified in biologically male individuals.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>I do not think the phrase &quot;biologically female/male&quot; in an SCP has ever resulted in anything good. The fixation on biology isn't particularly necessary in the context of what you're doing, anyways, and adding a compulsive effect to amplify victim's desire to have sex with the fish comes with its own host of problems.</p>
<blockquote>
<p>Yes, we were attacked by a madman at knifepoint.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>This whole thing is a significant amount of information pushed into a small space — why not create an addendum separate from the description to convey this in a more natural manner?</p>
</div>
</div>
</div>
<p>Overall it's got the general shape of an article that I'd like —&nbsp;it's short and it's got a little twist of expectations at the end — but I feel like the way you're communicating that twist lacks a level of nuance. Having the fish state their motivations is a bit of an anticlimax. In addition I can't really develop sympathy for the SCP since I get its motivations, but it's still manufacturing victims' desire to breed and overriding their consent. I feel like you're trying to get me to realize that these fish are just lonely but I'm not convinced.</p>

							</div>

								
					
