														</div>
			</div>
			<div class="content" id="post-content-4844989">
									

<p>Asked to take a look at this again.</p>
<blockquote>
<p>The Foundation quickly tracks them down, finding that various smaller groups are in possession of these devices and have been exploiting the enhanced children for some time, with one particular incident being the usage of a man born with an anomalous power unit in his torso to power a weapons production facility.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>So I'm not sure I can understand the connective tissue here. Even if a group of civilians were aware of the object's properties, how would that result in the exploitation that you're describing? It seems like a drastic jump.</p>
<p>I like the direction you're going, but I feel like you're lacking a central theme to all this. The baby-making machine seems entirely disconnected from the narrative about military operations against grassroots civilian groups. Maybe you could focus in on what the lives of these cyborgs are like a little more, use the anomaly as a stand-in for the traumas of the circumstances under which we're born, have the Foundation spy on a group therapy session where one of these adult instances is venting. There's some emotional work you could do there.</p>

							</div>

								
					
